{#
  PLATFORM BASELINE
  =================
  Fabric-wide switch settings: local user, AAA, logging, timestamps, clock,
  VTY/console, routing and multicast globals, archive, VTP, spanning-tree,
  errdisable, UDLD, CoPP class-maps and policy, licensing, redundancy, Vlan1,
  the management VRF and port, and NTP.

  APPLIED, NOT RENDERED. This is pushed once at onboarding by
  tasks/04_apply_baseline.yml via 'ios_config', which merges: it installs what
  is missing and leaves everything else alone. acm_evpn no longer renders any
  of it -- the candidate preserves whatever the device reports instead.

  That move fixed two things. It is set once for the life of a switch and only
  revisited on a deliberate fabric-wide event (rotating credentials, changing a
  timezone), so re-rendering it on every overlay change was pure churn: this is
  where nearly all the residual ACM diff came from, because IOS-XE renders back
  platform defaults this template does not model. And the readiness assertions
  in 02_check.yml can now hard-fail on these lines honestly -- while acm_evpn
  still rendered them, a failure blocked the very deploy that would have
  installed them.

  Anything IOS-XE hides from 'show running-config' MUST live here rather than
  in the acm_evpn render, for the same reason SNMPv3 does: a full-replace
  candidate can never converge on a line the device will not read back.

  The corollary, learned the expensive way: if such a line is also POINTLESS,
  drop it rather than finding a home for it. The VTP password and version were
  both removed on exactly that basis -- unverifiable, and meaningless in
  transparent mode.

  Device-native hardware lines (hw-module / switch / power) are deliberately
  absent: they are facts about the chassis, never something to push, and the
  candidate preserves them from the device.
#}
! --- 2. LOCAL USER & SSH ---
! The password below is injected securely from Infisical.
{#
  Prefer a pre-hashed secret. 'secret 0 <plaintext>' is re-hashed by the device
  to type 9, so the candidate never matches the running config: every deploy
  shows the username as changed and rewrites the password on a live device.
  Generate the hash once on any switch and store it in Infisical:
      username tmp privilege 15 secret <plaintext>
      show running-config | include ^username tmp
  then copy the $9$... value into ios_password_secret_hash.
#}
{% if local_admin_secret_hash %}
username {{ local_admin_user }} privilege 15 secret 9 {{ local_admin_secret_hash }}
{% else %}
username {{ local_admin_user }} privilege 15 secret 0 {{ local_admin_password }}
{% endif %}
!
aaa new-model
aaa authentication login default local
aaa authorization exec default local
!
ip scp server enable
!
! --- 3. BASELINE CONFIGURATION ---
logging buffered 1000000
service timestamps debug datetime msec localtime
service timestamps log datetime msec localtime
{# '10 0', not '+10': IOS stores hours and minutes separately and
   re-renders it that way, so '+10' never matches and churns. #}
clock timezone AEST 10 0
clock summer-time AEDT recurring 1 Sun Oct 2:00 1 Sun Apr 3:00
!
{#
  Every VTY line has to be hardened, not just the first five. A line outside
  what this template renders is reset to platform defaults by 'acm replace',
  and on modern IOS-XE that default is 'transport input none' -- so once VTY
  0-4 are occupied, further SSH sessions are refused. With forks set high, a
  fabric-wide run can occupy five lines on its own.
#}
line vty 0 4
 exec-timeout 60 0
 privilege level 15
 logging synchronous
 transport preferred none
 transport input ssh
line vty 5 {{ vty_line_max }}
 exec-timeout 60 0
 privilege level 15
 logging synchronous
 transport preferred none
 transport input ssh
line con 0
 exec-timeout 60 0
 privilege level 15
 logging synchronous
 transport preferred none
 stopbits 1
!

{#
  'ip routing' only when the switch is not already routing.

  It is default-ON for the C9400/9500/9600, which never print it, so pushing it
  there changed nothing and was reported on every single run -- the last
  remaining line in the apply count. The C9300 has it off by default and does
  need it.

  The condition is evidence-based rather than platform-based, so there is no
  model table to maintain: skip only when routing is demonstrably already on
  (see 01_gather.yml). An explicit 'no ip routing' forces it back, and a switch
  that shows no evidence either way gets the line -- harmless where it is
  already the default.
#}
{# default(false) so that if these facts are ever missing the line is emitted
   rather than the render failing -- pushing it is harmless. #}
{% if (_readiness_routing_disabled | default(false) | bool)
      or not (_readiness_routing_enabled | default(false) | bool) %}
ip routing
{% endif %}
ipv6 unicast-routing
ip multicast-routing
ip pim ssm default
ip pim rp-address {{ fabric_rp_address }} override
ip pim register-source {{ vtep_source_intf }}
!
ip cef load-sharing algorithm include-ports source destination
ipv6 cef load-sharing algorithm include-ports source destination
ip multicast multipath s-g-hash next-hop-based
port-channel load-balance src-dst-mixed-ip-port
!
ip tcp path-mtu-discovery
ip tcp mss 8000
{# 'ip tcp window-size 131072' removed, and NOT replaced by a check.

   It was pushed here first, which changed nothing on a switch already at the
   default and was never rendered back -- an unconvergeable no-op reported on
   every run. It then became a negative check in 02_check.yml, which was no
   better: the default is platform-specific, so on a real fabric that fired on
   all seven switches at once for a value correct on each of them.

   Neither pushed nor checked now. It is a per-platform setting and the fabric
   does not need to agree on it. #}
!
no ip forward-protocol nd
no ip forward-protocol udp tftp
no ip telnet comport enable
no ip http server
ip http authentication local
no ip http secure-server
!
ip ftp passive
ip tftp blocksize 8192
ip ssh bulk-mode 131072
ip ssh source-interface {{ vtep_source_intf }}
ntp source {{ vtep_source_intf }}
ip domain lookup source-interface {{ vtep_source_intf }}
ip http client source-interface {{ vtep_source_intf }}
ip radius source-interface {{ vtep_source_intf }}
logging source-interface {{ vtep_source_intf }}
snmp-server trap-source {{ vtep_source_intf }}
!
ntp server {{ ntp_server_1 }}
ntp server {{ ntp_server_2 }}
!
{#
  SNMPv3 -- the ACL, the group and the user -- moved to roles/underlay_readiness.

  'snmp-server user' is hidden from 'show running-config', so this block could
  never converge: it was pushed, accepted, and then appeared in the next deploy
  diff again because the device would not read it back. It also wrote the auth
  and priv passwords in plaintext into every rendered candidate.

  Dropping it from the render does not remove it from a switch. It stops being
  template-owned, so the candidate now preserves whatever the device already
  has -- and the readiness role asserts it is there.

  'snmp-server trap-source' above stays: it is source-interface hygiene along
  with the ip http / ip radius / logging lines, not an SNMP credential.
#}
{#
  System MTU. 9100 is unchanged as the value a new switch is brought to -- it
  covers jumbo tenant frames as well as the 50 bytes of VXLAN header -- but it
  is now rendered ONLY for a switch that has not been given a value of its own.

  It used to be unconditional. That was invisible on a fabric already at 9100,
  and is why it went unnoticed, but ios_config merges: on a switch running
  'system mtu 1600' the 9100 line is missing from the running config, so every
  readiness run would push it and bounce every interface on the box. Harmless
  while the check demanded 9100 anyway; not harmless now that any value at or
  above readiness_system_mtu_min is valid and deliberate.

  Below the floor is, in practice, a switch that has never been configured:
  1500 is the platform default and IOS-XE does not render it back. Above the
  floor the value is left exactly as found.

  Guarded on the fact being defined too -- an unreachable switch never reached
  the gather, and rendering must not fail before the connection does.
#}
{% if _readiness_system_mtu is defined and (_readiness_system_mtu | int) < (readiness_system_mtu_min | int) %}
system mtu 9100
!
{% endif %}
archive
 log config
  logging enable
  logging size 500
{# 'hidekeys' removed because it is the default, so it never renders back and
   was re-pushed forever. Unlike 'ip tcp window-size' above it IS still checked
   negatively in 02_check.yml, and that distinction is the point: 'no hidekeys'
   writes credentials into archived configs on every platform, so it is wrong
   everywhere rather than merely different between platforms. #}
!
vtp mode transparent
vtp domain {{ vtp_domain }}
{# 'vtp version 3' and 'vtp password' both removed entirely. Neither is a
   prerequisite for transparent mode or the domain, neither is rendered into the
   running config, and in transparent mode neither has any functional effect
   here -- so both were unverifiable lines reported changed on every run for no
   benefit. Only the two lines above matter, and both converge normally.

   Switches that already carry a VTP password keep it: it is invisible to
   'show running-config', so nothing preserves it and nothing removes it. #}
!
spanning-tree mode rapid-pvst
spanning-tree logging
spanning-tree portfast default
spanning-tree portfast bpduguard default
spanning-tree extend system-id
spanning-tree vlan 1-4094 priority 0
!
errdisable detect cause security-violation shutdown vlan
errdisable detect cause bpduguard shutdown vlan
errdisable recovery cause udld
errdisable recovery cause bpduguard
errdisable recovery cause security-violation
errdisable recovery cause channel-misconfig
errdisable recovery cause pagp-flap
errdisable recovery cause dtp-flap
errdisable recovery cause link-flap
errdisable recovery cause psecure-violation
errdisable recovery cause port-mode-failure
errdisable recovery cause dhcp-rate-limit
errdisable recovery cause mac-limit
errdisable recovery cause storm-control
errdisable recovery cause inline-power
errdisable recovery cause arp-inspection
errdisable recovery interval 120
!
udld aggressive
udld recovery
udld recovery interval 120
!
